 succeeded in 0ms:
   220	v1** (filed forward — §7).
   221	
   222	### 4.3 Wire-size caps — 5 M cells / 64 MiB
   223	
   224	A UDF cannot force an unbounded allocation across the boundary. The grid codec caps
   225	both cell-count and byte-length **before** allocating:
   226	
   227	```rust
   228	// crates/ql-udf/src/codec.rs:132
   229	pub const MAX_GRID_CELLS: usize = 5_000_000;
   230	// crates/ql-udf/src/codec.rs:137
   231	pub const MAX_GRID_BYTES: usize = crate::frame::MAX_FRAME_LEN as usize;  // 64 MiB
   232	```
   233	
   234	`encode_grid` rejects argument grids over the cell cap (`CodecError::GridTooManyCells`),
   235	`decode_grid` rejects return bytes over the byte cap before reading
   236	(`CodecError::GridTooManyBytes`) — both surface as `#VALUE!` + a `udf_grid_too_large`
   237	diagnostic (`crates/ql-exec/src/scalar.rs`, `map_udf_error`). The frame layer is the
   238	exact wire gate underneath:
   239	
   240	```rust
   241	// crates/ql-udf/src/frame.rs:88
   242	pub const MAX_FRAME_LEN: u32 = 64 * 1024 * 1024;   // rejected on read AND write before alloc
   243	```
   244	
   245	> **Coverage note (honest):** the real-worker grid-cap path is enforced and
   246	> unit-tested in `ql-udf`, but the in-process `MockWorker` bypasses the codec, so
   247	> there is no end-to-end real-worker grid-cap integration test yet. Filed forward
   248	> (§7).
   249	
   250	---
   251	
   252	## 5. Process lifecycle and the orphan caveat
   253	
   254	`WorkerProcess::drop` hard-stops and reaps the **direct** child, and **detaches** the
   255	reader thread rather than joining it:
   256	
   257	```rust
   258	// crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:108-127 (abridged)
   259	impl Drop for WorkerProcess {
   260	    fn drop(&mut self) {
   261	        let _ = self.child.kill();
   262	        let _ = self.child.wait();   // reaps the direct child (no zombie)
   263	        drop(self.reader.take());    // DETACH the reader thread — do not join
   264	    }
   265	}
   266	```
   267	
   268	The detach is deliberate: if a UDF spawned a **grandchild** that inherited the
   269	worker's stdout write-end, the pipe stays open after the direct child dies, so the
   270	reader's `read_frame` would block forever and joining it would deadlock the engine on
   271	the hard-cancel path.
   272	
   273	**The caveat:** a UDF that spawns its own subprocess creates an **orphan**. The
   274	grandchild is reparented to `init` (not killed, not waited), and the one reader thread
   275	+ fd it holds open leaks until the engine process exits. The proper fix —
   276	process-group / session kill so grandchildren die with the worker — needs a `libc`/
   277	`nix` dependency and is filed forward (§7). **In v1, a UDF can outlive its own cancel
   278	by forking.**
   279	
   280	---
   281	
   282	## 6. Type-conversion safety (the codec is a trust boundary)
   283	
   284	The Arrow-IPC decode path is treated as an untrusted input boundary, because the bytes
   285	come from a process running user code:
   286	
   287	- The decoder validates the exact 5-field tagged schema (`kind/num/str/bool/err`)
   288	  **before** any positional column access (`crates/ql-udf/src/codec.rs`,
   289	  `EXPECTED_FIELDS`) — a malformed batch is a clean `CodecError`, never an OOB panic.
   290	- **NaN / Inf are rejected on both sides.** Rust decode rejects non-finite numbers
   291	  (`crates/ql-udf/src/codec.rs:428`, `CodecError::NonFinite`); the Python encoder
   292	  rejects them at source (`crates/quantbook-py/python/quantbook/_codec.py:117-120`).
   293	  This preserves the engine invariant that `Value::Number` is always finite
   294	  (`crates/ql-types/src/value.rs`, `Value::number()` sanitizes to `#NUM!`).
   295	- Unknown cell `kind`, null payloads, trailing batches, and shape overflow are all
   296	  explicit errors (`crates/ql-udf/src/codec.rs`, `CodecError` variants).
   297	- A UDF exception never crashes the worker loop — it is caught and returned as a
   298	  `RAISE` control frame, surfacing as `#CALC!` + a `udf_raised` diagnostic
   299	  (`crates/quantbook-py/python/quantbook/worker.py`).
   300	
   301	The exhaustive Rust↔Python type-conversion matrix (every `Value` variant, all 15
   302	`ErrorValue` sigils, and the numpy/pandas dtype boundary) is pinned by
   303	`crates/ql-exec/tests/udf_type_matrix.rs` (`UDF-6-03`). Note the honest boundary that
   304	test documents: `numpy.float64` round-trips (it subclasses Python `float`), but
   305	`numpy.int64`, `numpy.bool_`, and pandas `Series`/`DataFrame` do **not** — the Python
   306	encoder only accepts `(int, float, bool, str, None, Err)` and raises a loud
   307	`TypeError` (surfaced as `#CALC!`) for anything else. There is no silent coercion.
   308	
   309	---
   310	
   311	## 7. What is NOT defended (the explicit boundary)
   312	
   313	Everything below is **out of scope for v1**. A UDF — i.e. arbitrary trusted-workspace
   314	Python — can do all of these, and the engine neither prevents nor detects them. This
   315	list is exhaustive as of 6.4B; searched the `ql-udf` crate, `worker.py`, and the napi
   316	binding for any corresponding restriction and found none unless noted.
   317	
   318	| Capability | v1 behavior | Why it's unguarded |
   319	|---|---|---|
   320	| **Filesystem** | Full read/write/delete as the engine's OS user | No chroot / no seccomp; subprocess inherits the host view |
   321	| **Network** | Arbitrary outbound (and inbound) sockets | No network namespace / no egress filter |
   322	| **Environment / secrets** | Reads & mutates all inherited env vars | Full env inherited (`process.rs:178-191`) |
   323	| **Module imports** | Imports any installed package, no allow-list | `importlib.import_module` is called directly (`worker.py`) |
   324	| **Memory** | Allocates until the OS OOM-kills the worker | No `rlimit`/cgroup memory cap (searched, absent) |
   325	| **CPU** | Bounded only by the 30 s/120 s *wall-clock* deadlines, not CPU quota | No `setrlimit(RLIMIT_CPU)` / no cgroup CPU cap |
   326	| **Subprocess spawning** | Spawns arbitrary children; they **orphan** on cancel (§5) | No process-group kill (needs `libc`/`nix`) |
   327	| **Persistence across cancel** | A forked grandchild survives worker-kill | Reparented to `init`, not in the kill set |
   328	
   329	The defensive perimeter that **is** in place: the workspace-trust double gate (§2),
   330	process/memory/crash isolation (§3), the kill-backed deadline + op-budget + wire caps
   331	(§4), reaped direct child (§5), and the hardened codec boundary (§6). Nothing more.
   332	
   333	---
   334	
   335	## 8. Hardening roadmap (filed forward — not in v1)
   336	
   337	These are known gaps, tracked for post-v1. None is a regression; each is an
   338	intentional v1 scope cut recorded here so the boundary stays honest:
   339	
   340	1. **OS-level sandbox.** seccomp-bpf syscall filter and/or namespaces + cgroup limits
   341	   (memory, CPU, pids) around the worker — the real fix for §7. Needs `libc`/`nix`.
   342	2. **Process-group / session kill** so a forked grandchild dies with the worker (§5),
   343	   closing the orphan + reader-thread/fd leak.
   344	3. **Host-configurable budgets.** Expose `UDF_OP_BUDGET` and per-function deadlines
   345	   over napi instead of the hard-coded 120 s / 30 s (§4).
   346	4. **End-to-end real-worker grid-cap test** (the `MockWorker` bypasses the codec, §4.3).
   347	5. **Egress / filesystem policy** for the eventual AI/data connectors at 6.5 — same
   348	   model, broader surface.
   349	
   350	---
   351	
   352	## 9. Operator guidance (v1)
   353	
   354	- **Enable Python UDFs only in workspaces you trust as fully as any program you would
   355	  run yourself.** The trust gate is a deliberate, informed decision — not a sandbox.
   356	- Treat a `.qbook` / workbook that ships UDF code like a script: review the Python
   357	  before trusting the workspace.
   358	- Do not run untrusted workbooks on machines with ambient credentials (cloud tokens in
   359	  env, SSH agents, writable production mounts) — a UDF inherits all of them (§1, §7).
   360	- A hung or runaway UDF is bounded in *engine* terms (deadline + budget) but can still
   361	  burn CPU/memory/IO on the host until killed, and a forked child can outlive the
   362	  cancel (§5). Watch host resources for workbooks with heavy UDFs.

 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# UDF & AI-connector trust model (v1)
     2	
     3	> **Status:** v1, Phase 6.4B (`UDF-6-04`). This document is the *honest* trust model
     4	> for the Python user-defined-function (UDF) subsystem as it actually ships — every
     5	> claim below is grounded in a `file:line` citation, not an aspiration. It is
     6	> written across 6.4A/6.4B (UDFs) and will be extended at 6.5 (SQL / data
     7	> connectors). **AI connectors are not available in v1** — the engine reserves the
     8	> `#AI_NOT_AVAILABLE_V1` sigil (`crates/ql-types/src/error.rs`, `ErrorValue::AINotAvailable`)
     9	> and ships no AI execution path; the §"What is NOT defended" boundary below is the
    10	> model any future connector will inherit.
    11	>
    12	> Engine paths are relative to the engine repo
    13	> (`quantbook-engine/`). IDE paths are in the sibling `quantlab/quantlab` repo
    14	> (branch `feat/visualise-v1`), noted inline.
    15	
    16	## 0. TL;DR — the one thing to know
    17	
    18	**A Python UDF runs arbitrary workspace Python code with the full privileges of the
    19	process that hosts the engine.** There is **no hard sandbox** — no seccomp, no
    20	namespaces, no `rlimit`, no chroot, no container. The v1 safety story is:
    21	
    22	1. a **trust gate** (the workspace must be explicitly trusted before any worker
    23	   spawns), plus
    24	2. **subprocess isolation** (the UDF runs in a *separate OS process* so a crash or a
    25	   hang cannot corrupt the engine's memory and can always be killed), plus
    26	3. **resource bounds we can enforce from the Rust side** without cooperation from the
    27	   Python code — a per-call deadline, a per-recalc-pass op-budget, and hard caps on
    28	   the size of data crossing the wire.
    29	
    30	That is the entire defensive perimeter. Inside it, a UDF can read your files, open
    31	network sockets, read your environment, import anything installed, and allocate
    32	memory until the OS kills it. **Only enable Python UDFs in workspaces whose code you
    33	trust as much as you trust any other program you would run on your machine.**
    34	
    35	---
    36	
    37	## 1. What executing a UDF means
    38	
    39	A registered Python UDF is dispatched by the formula evaluator
    40	(`crates/ql-exec/src/scalar.rs`, the `None` arm that looks up
    41	`registry.udf_handle(name)`) to an out-of-process Python **worker**. The worker is
    42	spawned as a plain child process:
    43	
    44	```rust
    45	// crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:171-176
    46	let mut cmd = Command::new(&self.config.python);
    47	cmd.arg("-m")
    48	    .arg(&self.config.module)        // e.g. `quantbook.worker`
    49	    .stdin(Stdio::piped())
    50	    .stdout(Stdio::piped())          // the length-prefixed Arrow-IPC protocol channel
    51	    .stderr(Stdio::inherit());       // worker stderr → engine stderr
    52	```
    53	
    54	The worker imports the user's module by name with **no allow-list or validation**:
    55	
    56	```python
    57	# crates/quantbook-py/python/quantbook/worker.py (importlib.import_module(mod))
    58	```
    59	
    60	The child **inherits the engine process's full environment** — `PYTHONPATH` and every
    61	other variable (`crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:178-191`). It therefore runs with the
    62	same OS user, the same filesystem view, the same network reachability, and the same
    63	environment secrets as the engine host. This is by design for v1 (a developer tool
    64	running the developer's own analysis code); it is also the single most important fact
    65	in this document.
    66	
    67	---
    68	
    69	## 2. The trust boundary (two gates)
    70	
    71	Spawning a worker is gated **twice** — once in the IDE (which owns the concept of a
    72	"workspace") and once in the engine (which owns the session lifecycle). The engine
    73	itself has **no notion of a workspace**; the `worker_untrusted_workspace` error code
    74	is IDE-only and is never emitted by the engine
    75	(`crates/ql-bindings-node/src/lib.rs:5131-5134`).
    76	
    77	### 2.1 IDE gate — workspace trust (the *double* gate)
    78	
    79	`extensions/quantlab/src/quantbook/udfWorker.ts` (in `quantlab/quantlab`) refuses to
    80	spawn a worker unless the workspace is trusted by **both** trust authorities:
    81	
    82	```ts
    83	// udfWorker.ts:198-200
    84	const isWorkspaceTrusted =
    85	    vscode.workspace.isTrusted &&
    86	    TrustManager.getInstance().isWorkspaceTrusted(opts.workspaceUri);
    87	```
    88	
    89	- `vscode.workspace.isTrusted` — VS Code Restricted Mode. In Restricted Mode,
    90	  extensions MUST NOT execute workspace code.
    91	- `TrustManager...isWorkspaceTrusted(...)` — QuantLab's own per-workspace trust store.
    92	
    93	Both must be true. The gate runs **before** the interpreter is even resolved — an
    94	untrusted workspace never stats a path or spawns a subprocess
    95	(`udfWorker.ts:202-212`). When the gate fails, the pure planner
    96	`planUdfWorkerConfig` returns the IDE-only `[worker_untrusted_workspace]` code and the
    97	caller shows a non-fatal notice (`udfWorker.ts:117-124`). The interpreter is also
    98	verified to be Python ≥ 3.9 before use (`udfWorker.ts:44`, the worker is
    99	3.9-compatible).
   100	
   101	This double gate exists because a workspace can be trusted in QuantLab's own store yet
   102	opened in VS Code Restricted Mode — and a UDF worker runs arbitrary workspace Python,
   103	so the stricter of the two authorities must win (6.4-3d Step-5 audit fix, Codex HIGH).
   104	
   105	### 2.2 Engine gate — session lifecycle
   106	
   107	Injecting the spawned worker into a session is lifecycle-gated:
   108	
   109	```rust
   110	// crates/ql-exec/src/session.rs:453-459
   111	pub fn set_udf_worker_checked(&mut self, worker: Box<dyn UdfWorker + Send>) -> EngineResult<()> {
   112	    self.ensure_ready()?;            // rejects Closed / Faulted / New / Busy
   113	    self.udf_worker = Some(RefCell::new(worker));
   114	    Ok(())
   115	}
   116	```
   117	
   118	The napi binding spawns + handshakes the worker *outside* the session lock, then
   119	injects it *under* the lock through this checked setter — so a session that closes or
   120	faults during the spawn rejects the worker (and the caller drops it, killing the child)
   121	(`crates/ql-bindings-node/src/lib.rs:5172-5181`).
   122	
   123	**What the engine gate does NOT do:** it does not re-derive trust. The engine trusts
   124	that the host (the IDE) only calls `setUdfWorker` for a trusted workspace. A different
   125	napi host that ignores the IDE gate could spawn a worker against an untrusted
   126	workspace — the trust decision is structurally the host's. This is acceptable for v1
   127	(the only host is the QuantLab IDE, which double-gates) and is called out here so no
   128	future host assumes the engine enforces workspace trust.
   129	
   130	---
   131	
   132	## 3. Process isolation — what it buys, what it does not
   133	
   134	The UDF runs in a **separate OS process**, which gives us the properties we *do* rely
   135	on:
   136	
   137	- **Memory isolation.** A UDF cannot read or corrupt the engine's address space; the
   138	  only channel is the length-prefixed Arrow-IPC pipe.
   139	- **Crash isolation.** A worker segfault / `os._exit` / fatal exception takes down the
   140	  worker, not the engine; the next call lazily respawns
   141	  (`crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs`, respawn on dead worker).
   142	- **Hard cancel.** Because it is a separate process, it can always be killed (§4) —
   143	  the only honest cancel under a GIL-only CPython.
   144	
   145	It does **not** give us any OS-level confinement. Searched
   146	`crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs`, `crates/ql-udf/src/lib.rs`, and
   147	`crates/ql-udf/Cargo.toml` for `seccomp`, `namespace`, `rlimit`, `setrlimit`,
   148	`prctl`, `cgroup`, `chroot`, `libc`, `nix` — **none present.** The worker is an
   149	ordinary subprocess with inherited privileges.
   150	
   151	---
   152	
   153	## 4. Cancellation and resource bounds (Rust-enforced, no Python cooperation)
   154	
   155	Under a GIL-only CPython there is no way to cooperatively interrupt a running UDF — a
   156	`while True: pass` cannot be unwound from outside. So **the only cancel is killing the
   157	process**, and every bound below is ultimately backed by that kill.
   158	
   159	### 4.1 Per-call deadline — 30 s
   160	
   161	```rust
   162	// crates/ql-exec/src/scalar.rs:66
   163	const UDF_CALL_DEADLINE: Duration = Duration::from_secs(30);
   164	```
   165	
   166	The worker's `call()` checks the clock before each frame receive and **kills the
   167	worker** when the deadline passes; a late `RETURN` is dropped:
   168	
   169	```rust
   170	// crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:333-336 (and the recv_timeout arm at :382-385)
   171	if Instant::now() >= deadline_at {
   172	    self.kill_worker();
   173	    return Err(UdfError::Timeout(deadline));
   174	}
   175	```
   176	
   177	The cell becomes `#TIMEOUT!` (`ErrorValue::Timeout`) and a `udf_timeout` diagnostic is
   178	recorded.
   179	
   180	### 4.2 Per-recalc-pass op-budget — 120 s (the N×30s stall fix)
   181	
   182	A single recompute can touch many UDF cells; 30 s each would let N cells stall the
   183	pass for N×30 s. The session arms a **whole-pass budget**:
   184	
   185	```rust
   186	// crates/ql-exec/src/scalar.rs:80
   187	pub(crate) const UDF_OP_BUDGET: Duration = Duration::from_secs(120);
   188	```
   189	
   190	Each call's effective deadline is clamped to `min(30 s, time remaining in the pass)`:
   191	
   192	```rust
   193	// crates/ql-exec/src/scalar.rs:93-103
   194	fn effective_udf_deadline(op_deadline: Option<Instant>, now: Instant) -> Option<Duration> {
   195	    match op_deadline {
   196	        None => Some(UDF_CALL_DEADLINE),
   197	        Some(dl) => match dl.checked_duration_since(now) {
   198	            Some(remaining) if !remaining.is_zero() => Some(remaining.min(UDF_CALL_DEADLINE)),
   199	            _ => None,                       // budget exhausted
   200	        },
   201	    }
   202	}
   203	```
   204	
   205	Once the budget is exhausted, remaining UDF cells in the pass are **not dispatched at
   206	all** — they short-circuit to `#TIMEOUT!` with a distinct diagnostic, so the recompute
   207	pass is bounded regardless of UDF count:
   208	
   209	```rust
   210	// crates/ql-exec/src/scalar.rs:1194-1200
   211	let Some(effective) = effective_udf_deadline(env.udf_op_deadline(), Instant::now()) else {
   212	    push_udf_cell_diagnostic(env, "udf_budget_exhausted",
   213	        "recompute UDF time budget exhausted before this cell; not dispatched".to_string());
   214	    return FunctionReturn::Scalar(Value::Error(ErrorValue::Timeout));
   215	};
   216	```
   217	
   218	The budget is armed only when a worker is attached; non-UDF / no-worker recompute is
   219	byte-for-byte unchanged (deadline `None`). The budget is **not host-configurable in
   220	v1** (filed forward — §7).

