   340	    /// Register first-class metadata for a function. The `canonical_name`
   341	    /// **must** be ASCII-uppercase (matches the parser's canonicalization);
   342	    /// rejecting a non-uppercase name with `Conflict` would be wrong
   343	    /// (that's bad input, not collision), so this asserts loud — the
   344	    /// caller is expected to pass already-canonical names per contract
   345	    /// §10.3 ("`register_function` normalizes + validates").
   346	    ///
   347	    /// Returns [`FunctionRegistryError::Conflict`] if a metadata entry
   348	    /// already exists for this name (built-in or prior UDF), per the
   349	    /// fail-loud collision rule in contract §10.3. This is the
   350	    /// non-panicking counterpart to [`Self::insert_or_panic`]'s
   351	    /// dispatch-side duplicate panic — UDFs register at runtime where
   352	    /// a panic would abort the host.
   353	    pub fn register_metadata(
   354	        &mut self,
   355	        meta: FunctionMetadata,
   356	    ) -> Result<(), FunctionRegistryError> {
   357	        assert!(
   358	            !meta.canonical_name.is_empty(),
   359	            "FunctionRegistry::register_metadata: canonical_name must not be empty"
   360	        );
   361	        assert!(
   362	            meta.canonical_name
   363	                .bytes()
   364	                .all(|b| !b.is_ascii_lowercase()),
   365	            "FunctionRegistry::register_metadata: canonical_name {:?} must be \
   366	             canonical upper-case (caller must normalize before calling)",
   367	            meta.canonical_name
   368	        );
   369	        if self.metadata.contains_key(&meta.canonical_name) {
   370	            return Err(FunctionRegistryError::Conflict {
   371	                name: meta.canonical_name,
   372	            });
   373	        }
   374	        self.metadata.insert(meta.canonical_name.clone(), meta);
   375	        // **6.4-1 (2026-05-28; H3):** bump the metadata generation counter
   376	        // so any `PlanCacheKey` minted before this call misses on next
   377	        // lookup. The bind cache invalidation is what closes contract
   378	        // §10.3's "re-extract deps" half — `CalcgraphSession`'s hooks
   379	        // already dirty + reschedule; the cache miss forces re-binding
   380	        // through `walk_plan_for_deps` against the current metadata so
   381	        // `FormulaDeps::is_volatile` / `functions_used` / `is_volatile`
   382	        // / `dep_shape`-driven routing all reflect the new entry. Only
   383	        // bumped on success — a `Conflict` returned above leaves the
   384	        // metadata table state and `fn_gen` both untouched.
   385	        self.fn_gen = self.fn_gen.saturating_add(1);
   386	        Ok(())
   387	    }
   388	
   389	    /// Remove metadata for `canonical_name`. Returns
   390	    /// [`FunctionRegistryError::NotFound`] if no entry exists — never a
   391	    /// silent no-op (per No-Fallbacks: a quiet remove would mask a typo
   392	    /// that leaves stale metadata associated with a since-renamed name).
   393	    /// Lookup uppercases the query for symmetry with [`Self::metadata`].
   394	    ///
   395	    /// **6.4-0 audit-fix (2026-05-28; Codex A LOW):** REFUSES to
   396	    /// remove metadata for any name that still has a dispatch entry
   397	    /// in [`Self::fns`]. Without this guard, an unregister-builtin
   398	    /// call would leave dispatch present + metadata absent — a state
   399	    /// the migration-shim invariant (every dispatched fn has metadata)
   400	    /// forbids, and which would silently regress the dep walker for
   401	    /// the affected built-in (unknown-fn semantics). The (6.4)
   402	    /// `WorkbookSession::unregister_function` trait method should
   403	    /// only ever target UDF names (no dispatch entry by construction);
   404	    /// this guard catches a programmer error at the registry layer
   405	    /// rather than silently corrupting the metadata invariant.
   406	    /// Returns [`FunctionRegistryError::Conflict`] (re-using the
   407	    /// existing variant — the conflict is between "remove" and
   408	    /// "dispatch-still-present").
   409	    pub fn unregister_metadata(
   410	        &mut self,
   411	        canonical_name: &str,
   412	    ) -> Result<(), FunctionRegistryError> {
   413	        let upper = canonical_name.to_ascii_uppercase();
   414	        // Guard: refuse to break the dispatch-has-metadata invariant.
   415	        if self.fns.contains_key(upper.as_str()) {
   416	            return Err(FunctionRegistryError::Conflict {
   417	                name: format!(
   418	                    "{canonical_name} is a registered built-in (dispatch entry present); \
   419	                     unregister_metadata must only target UDF metadata"
   420	                ),
   421	            });
   422	        }
   423	        if self.metadata.remove(&upper).is_some() {
   424	            // **6.4-2 (2026-05-28):** symmetric handle cleanup. The
   425	            // builtin-guard above ensures the metadata being removed is
   426	            // UDF-only, so `udf_handles` MAY or MAY NOT carry a matching
   427	            // entry:
   428	            // - YES if the metadata was registered via
   429	            //   [`Self::register_udf`] (the 6.4-2 trait-wiring path) —
   430	            //   handle inserted alongside.
   431	            // - NO if the metadata was registered via the lower-level
   432	            //   [`Self::register_metadata`] (e.g., a test-only path that
   433	            //   bypasses the trait method's combined registration).
   434	            // The `remove` returns `Option`; we ignore the return because
   435	            // a missing handle is NOT an error here — it just means the
   436	            // UDF was metadata-only. The metadata removal already pinned
   437	            // the success-path; the handle removal is a defense-in-depth
   438	            // cleanup that leaves the invariant
   439	            // "every key in `udf_handles` has a matching `metadata` entry"
   440	            // intact even if a future code path registers metadata-only.
   441	            self.udf_handles.remove(&upper);
   442	            // **6.4-1 (2026-05-28; H3):** symmetric `fn_gen` bump on
   443	            // successful removal. A `NotFound` returned below leaves the
   444	            // counter alone (metadata table unchanged → cache stays
   445	            // valid).
   446	            self.fn_gen = self.fn_gen.saturating_add(1);
   447	            Ok(())
   448	        } else {
   449	            Err(FunctionRegistryError::NotFound {
   450	                name: canonical_name.to_string(),
   451	            })
   452	        }
   453	    }
   454	
   455	    /// **6.4-2 (2026-05-28):** combined UDF-metadata + dispatch-handle
   456	    /// registration. Used by `WorkbookSession::register_function` (the
   457	    /// 6.4-2 trait-wiring sub-increment); built-ins use the metadata-only
   458	    /// path at boot via [`register_builtin_metadata`].
   459	    ///
   460	    /// **Atomicity:** the metadata insertion happens first (via
   461	    /// [`Self::register_metadata`]); only on its `Ok` does the handle
   462	    /// land in [`Self::udf_handles`]. A `Conflict` from
   463	    /// `register_metadata` short-circuits BEFORE the handle insert, so
   464	    /// the registry never observes a state with a handle but no
   465	    /// metadata (the inverse — metadata without handle — is the normal
   466	    /// state for built-ins and metadata-only test registrations).
   467	    /// `HashMap::insert` itself cannot fail, so once `register_metadata`
   468	    /// succeeds, the handle insertion is infallible.
   469	    ///
   470	    /// **Single `fn_gen` bump:** the bump happens inside
   471	    /// [`Self::register_metadata`]; the handle insertion does NOT add
   472	    /// another bump. Bind cache invalidation is keyed by metadata mutation,
   473	    /// not by handle mutation — UDF dispatch consults the handle table at
   474	    /// eval time, not at bind time.
   475	    ///
   476	    /// Returns the same [`FunctionRegistryError`] taxonomy as
   477	    /// [`Self::register_metadata`]: `Conflict` on duplicate canonical name
   478	    /// (built-in or existing UDF). The `WorkbookSession` trait method
   479	    /// translates this via `map_function_registry_err` to the
   480	    /// contract-§10.3 `EngineError{class:Conflict, code:"function_exists"}`.
   481	    pub fn register_udf(
   482	        &mut self,
   483	        meta: FunctionMetadata,
   484	        handle: FunctionImplHandle,
   485	    ) -> Result<(), FunctionRegistryError> {
   486	        // Stash the canonical name BEFORE `register_metadata` moves `meta`
   487	        // into the table — needed as the `udf_handles` key.
   488	        let canonical_name = meta.canonical_name.clone();
   489	        self.register_metadata(meta)?;
   490	        // `register_metadata` succeeded → metadata is in the table and
   491	        // `fn_gen` ticked. Insert the handle under the same canonical key.
   492	        // HashMap::insert can't fail; the prior value (if any) was already
   493	        // ruled out by the `Conflict` check inside `register_metadata`.
   494	        self.udf_handles.insert(canonical_name, handle);
   495	        Ok(())
   496	    }
   497	
   498	    /// **6.4-2 (2026-05-28):** read the dispatch handle for a UDF by
   499	    /// canonical name (case-insensitive — uppercases the query for
   500	    /// symmetry with [`Self::metadata`]). Returns `None` for built-ins
   501	    /// (no handle), for unknown names, and for UDFs registered
   502	    /// metadata-only via the lower-level [`Self::register_metadata`].
   503	    ///
   504	    /// 6.4-3's worker dispatcher will call this at eval time to route
   505	    /// `RegisteredFn::Udf(_)` calls to the Python worker process; v1
   506	    /// substrate just owns the storage.
   507	    pub fn udf_handle(&self, canonical_name: &str) -> Option<FunctionImplHandle> {
   508	        let upper = canonical_name.to_ascii_uppercase();
   509	        self.udf_handles.get(upper.as_str()).copied()
   510	    }

context compacted
codex
**Findings**

**MEDIUM** — `quantbook-engine/crates/ql-exec/src/session.rs:912`, `quantbook-engine/crates/ql-exec/src/session.rs:1265`  
`run_recalc` arms the UDF op budget correctly at `session.rs:993-1004`, but other full recompute paths with a preserved worker do not. `rematerialize()` and `open()` call `recompute_all_preserving_saved_udf()` through `with_runtime_no_oplog` without `arm_udf_op_deadline`, while formula eval will still dispatch UDFs through `workbook_runtime/recompute.rs:963-973`. A workbook opened or rematerialized with many slow UDF formulas can therefore take roughly `N * UDF_CALL_DEADLINE`, not the documented 120s pass budget.  
Fix: factor recompute entrypoints so every worker-attached full recompute arms the same op deadline, or explicitly document and test the narrower `run_recalc`-only bound.

**LOW** — `quantbook-engine/crates/ql-udf/src/codec.rs:263`, `quantbook-engine/crates/ql-udf/src/codec.rs:269`  
`encode_grid_capped` checks the byte cap only after Arrow IPC serialization has already populated `buf`. Cell count is prechecked, but byte size is not allocation-preventing on the encode path. This does not let a malicious worker bypass the decode/frame receive cap, but it does mean a large outbound grid can force transient memory growth before `GridTooManyBytes`.  
Fix: add a preflight byte estimate for variable payloads, use a bounded writer, or update the claim to say the encode byte cap is post-serialization.

**LOW** — `quantbook-engine/docs/security/udf-ai-connectors.md:224`, `quantbook-engine/docs/security/udf-ai-connectors.md:225`  
The security doc overstates the allocation guarantees: “caps both cell-count and byte-length before allocating” is not true as written. Decode byte cap is checked before Arrow parsing at `codec.rs:357-365`, and the frame cap rejects before payload allocation at `frame.rs:116-124`; however decode’s cell-count cap runs after Arrow has built a `RecordBatch` at `codec.rs:366-404`, and encode’s byte cap is post-buffer allocation at `codec.rs:263-277`.  
Fix: rewrite this section to distinguish frame payload allocation, Arrow decode allocation, engine `Vec<Value>` allocation, and encode-side transient buffers.

**LOW** — `quantbook-engine/crates/ql-exec/src/scalar.rs:1205`  
The 1x1-grid scalar return path uses `unwrap_or(Value::Error(ErrorValue::Calc))`. In the current codec this should be unreachable for a valid `ArrayValue`, but it silently converts an invariant violation into `#CALC!` without a diagnostic. That violates the no-fallbacks discipline for UDF paths.  
Fix: replace with an explicit `match`; either emit a diagnostic such as `udf_protocol`/`udf_codec` before returning `#CALC!`, or assert the invariant with a clear panic if malformed `ArrayValue` is impossible by construction.

**LOW** — `quantbook-engine/crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:115`, `quantbook-engine/crates/ql-udf/src/process.rs:116`  
`WorkerProcess::drop` swallows both `child.kill()` and `child.wait()` errors. Drop cannot return an error, but silently ignoring lifecycle failures weakens the “kill + reap” claim and makes failures invisible during hardening tests.  
Fix: log these failures at minimum, and consider an explicit shutdown method returning `Result` for non-drop paths.

**INFO** — `quantbook-engine/crates/ql-exec/tests/udf_type_matrix.rs:163`  
The real-worker matrix covers the degenerate `0x3` grid but not `3x0` or `0x0`. Codec unit coverage exists elsewhere, but the out-of-process round-trip matrix is incomplete for those shapes.  
Fix: add real-worker fixtures/assertions for `3x0` and `0x0`.

**Scope Checks With No Additional Findings**

Grid decode/frame caps look sound on the receive path: `frame.rs:116-124` rejects oversized frames before payload allocation; `codec.rs:357-365` checks grid bytes before Arrow parsing; `codec.rs:392-409` uses `checked_mul` and rejects `cell_count > max_cells` before allocating the engine cell vec. Exact cap equality is allowed consistently.

Within `run_recalc`, the op budget logic is correct: `effective_udf_deadline` at `scalar.rs:93-103` treats exhausted deadlines as no dispatch, clamps each call to 30s, and preserves the `None` path for no-worker recomputes.

For UDF timeout/cancel, I did not find a no-late-commit path. Timed-out workers are killed and dropped, stale call IDs are ignored, and respawn uses a fresh process/channel. Dead workers surface as `WorkerDied` rather than hanging in the normal pipe/reader paths.

FF-1 is actually enforced before registry mutation at `session.rs:2683-2708`. The parser-probe gate rejects injection-style names and accepts parser-valid function names, including dotted names and CellRef-lexed cases.

FF-2 diagnostics are threaded into transaction commit at `transaction.rs:441-459`, and the new test asserts one recorded `udf_raised` diagnostic. The older constructors remain no-diagnostics paths by construction.

The UDF-6-03 type matrix uses a real `ProcessWorker`, not a mock. The numpy/pandas skip guards are honest, and the TypeError assertions are not trivially passing.

I could not independently verify the IDE-side double gate cited in `docs/security/udf-ai-connectors.md` because the sibling `quantlab/quantlab` repo is not present here. The engine-side checked gate is present.

**Verdict: SHIP-WITH-FIXES**

No HIGH issue showed up: the critical return-frame allocation path is capped before payload allocation, and I did not find a late-return commit bug. However, the 120s op-budget claim is too broad because open/rematerialize recomputes are not budget-armed, and the security doc overstates allocation guarantees. Fix those before treating 6.4B as closed.
